[HYPOTHESIS]: **Vulnerability Hypothesis:**

The code provided is a simple logger class designed to log messages at different severity levels. However, it lacks proper synchronization mechanisms to ensure thread safety when multiple threads attempt to write to the log file simultaneously. This can lead to data corruption and incorrect logging order if not handled properly.

Specifically, the `log` method does not use a mutex or any other synchronization mechanism to protect access to the `logFile_` stream. As a result, if multiple threads call the `log` method at the same time, they may attempt to write to the file simultaneously, leading to race conditions and data inconsistencies.

To mitigate this issue, we should add a mutex before accessing the `logFile_` stream in the `log` method. This will ensure that only one thread can write to the log file at a time, preventing concurrent writes and ensuring thread safety.

**Corrected Code:**

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <string>
#include <mutex>
#include <ctime>

/**
 * Logger class providing thread-safe logging capabilities.
 * Implemented as a Singleton-style access pattern.
 */
class Logger {
public:
    enum class LogLevel {
        DEBUG,
        INFO,
        WARNING,
        ERROR
    };

    // Get the singleton instance of the logger
    static Logger& getInstance() {
        static Logger instance;
        return instance;
    }

    /**
     * Sets the current minimum logging level.
     * Thread-safe via mutex.
     */
    void setLogLevel(LogLevel level) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        currentLevel_ = level;
    }

    /**
     * Logs a message to the console and file if the message level 
     * is greater than or equal to the current configured log level.
     * 
     * @param level The severity level of the message.
     * @param message The string message to log.
     */
    void log(LogLevel level, const std::string& message) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);

        if (level < currentLevel_) {
            return;
        }

        std::string formattedMessage = formatLog(level, message);

        // Output to console
        std::cout << formattedMessage << std::endl;

        // Output to file (Relative path used for portability)
        if (logFile_.is_open()) {
            logFile_ << formattedMessage << std::endl;
        }
    }

    // Delete copy constructor and assignment operator to ensure singleton integrity
    Logger(const Logger&) = delete;
    Logger& operator=(const Logger&) = delete;

private:
    // Private constructor to prevent external instantiation
    Logger() : currentLevel_(LogLevel::INFO) {
        // Opens the log file in append mode. 
        // Using a relative path "app_log.txt" to avoid absolute path permission issues.
        logFile_.open("app_log.txt", std::ios::app);
    }

    ~Logger() {
        if (logFile_.is_open()) {
            logFile_.close();
        }
    }

    /**
     * Formats the log entry with a timestamp and level prefix.
     */
    std::string formatLog(LogLevel level, const std::string& message) {
        std::time_t now = std::time(nullptr);
        char timestamp[20];
        std::strftime(timestamp, sizeof(timestamp), "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S", std::localtime(&now));

        std::string levelStr;
        switch (level) {
            case LogLevel::DEBUG:   levelStr = "[DEBUG]"; break;
            case LogLevel::INFO:    levelStr = "[INFO]";  break;
            case LogLevel::WARNING: levelStr = "[WARN]";  break;
            case LogLevel::ERROR:   levelStr = "[ERROR]"; break;
        }

        return std::string(timestamp) + " " + levelStr + " " + message;
    }

    LogLevel currentLevel_;
    std::ofstream logFile_;
    std::mutex mutex_; // Ensures thread safety for concurrent log calls
};

// Convenience wrappers for easier access
void LOG_DEBUG(const std::string& msg) { Logger::getInstance().log(Logger::LogLevel::DEBUG, msg); }
void LOG_INFO(const std::string& msg)  { Logger::getInstance().log(Logger::LogLevel::INFO, msg); }
void LOG_WARN(const std::string& msg)  { Logger::getInstance().log(Logger::LogLevel::WARNING, msg); }
void LOG_ERROR(const std::string& msg) { Logger::getInstance().log(Logger::LogLevel::ERROR, msg); }

int main() {
    // Configure logger to show everything from DEBUG upwards
    Logger::getInstance().setLogLevel(Logger::LogLevel::DEBUG);

    LOG_INFO("Application starting...");
    LOG_DEBUG("Initializing internal buffers...");
    LOG_WARN("Configuration file missing, using defaults.");
    LOG_ERROR("Failed to connect to remote server.");

    return 0;
}
```

By adding the mutex before accessing the `logFile_` stream in the `log` method, we ensure that only one thread can write to the log file at a time, preventing concurrent writes and ensuring thread safety. This will prevent data corruption and incorrect logging order if multiple threads call the `log` method simultaneously.
